BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 1 - Introduction to computer networks) Tj T* () Tj T* (A computer network is a group of devices that can exchange data with one another.) Tj T* (The devices may be personal computers, phones, printers, servers or sensors, and) Tj T* (they are connected by cables or by radio waves. The main reason for building a) Tj T* (network is to share: files, printers, an internet connection and applications can) Tj T* (all be used by many people at the same time instead of being copied to every) Tj T* (machine.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Every network is built from the same basic ingredients. Hosts are the devices that) Tj T* (send and receive data. Links are the media that carry the signals between them.) Tj T* (Intermediate devices such as switches and routers decide where the data goes next.) Tj T* (Finally, protocols are the agreed rules that describe how a message is formatted,) Tj T* (addressed, sent and acknowledged. Without common protocols, equipment from different) Tj T* (manufacturers could not work together.) Tj T* () Tj T* (These notes follow the data on its journey. We start with the cables and the radio) Tj T* (signals, move up through addressing and routing, and finish with the services that) Tj T* (people use every day, such as the web and e-mail. Each chapter is short and ends) Tj T* (with the key ideas to remember. The notes are written for beginners, so every new) Tj T* (term is defined when it first appears, and examples are kept small enough to be) Tj T* (checked by hand.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A useful habit is to follow one message from the sender to the receiver and ask at) Tj T* (each step which device handles it and which rule it follows. This habit will make) Tj T* (the later chapters on layers and routing much easier to understand.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
