BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 2 - Types of networks) Tj T* () Tj T* (Networks are often classified by the area they cover. A personal area network, or) Tj T* (PAN, connects devices around one person, for example a phone and a wireless headset,) Tj T* (over a few metres. A local area network, or LAN, covers a single building or campus) Tj T* (such as a home, a school or an office. A metropolitan area network, or MAN, links) Tj T* (several sites inside a city. A wide area network, or WAN, spans cities, countries or) Tj T* (even continents and usually relies on lines rented from telecommunication companies.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The Internet is not one network but a network of networks. Thousands of independent) Tj T* (organisations, called autonomous systems, operate their own networks and agree to) Tj T* (exchange traffic with each other. Internet service providers connect homes and) Tj T* (companies to this global mesh. When you open a web page, your request may cross the) Tj T* (networks of several different operators before it reaches the server.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Networks can also be classified by who controls them. A private network, such as a) Tj T* (company intranet, is reserved for its owner and its authorised users. A public) Tj T* (network, such as the Internet, can be reached by anyone with a connection. An) Tj T* (extranet gives selected outside partners limited access to part of a private) Tj T* (network.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Another distinction is the architecture. In a client-server network, dedicated) Tj T* (servers provide services to clients that request them. In a peer-to-peer network,) Tj T* (every host can act as both client and server. Most of what we use daily, from web) Tj T* (browsing to streaming, follows the client-server model.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
