BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 3 - Network topologies) Tj T* () Tj T* (The topology of a network describes how its devices are arranged and connected. The) Tj T* (physical topology is the real layout of cables and devices, while the logical) Tj T* (topology describes the path that data actually follows. The two do not have to be) Tj T* (the same.) Tj T* () Tj T* (In a bus topology all devices share a single cable. It is cheap, but a break in the) Tj T* (cable stops the whole network and every device competes for the same medium. In a) Tj T* (ring topology each device is connected to exactly two neighbours and data travels) Tj T* (around the ring in one direction. In a star topology every device has its own cable) Tj T* (to a central switch. The star is by far the most common layout in modern offices and) Tj T* (homes, because a failing cable only disconnects one device and new devices are easy) Tj T* (to add.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A full mesh topology connects every device directly to every other device. It is) Tj T* (very reliable because there are many alternative paths, but the number of links) Tj T* (grows quickly: a full mesh of ten devices needs forty-five links. For this reason) Tj T* (full meshes are used only for a few critical connections, while a partial mesh is) Tj T* (common between routers on the Internet. A tree topology combines several star) Tj T* (networks by linking their central devices in a hierarchy, which is how large) Tj T* (buildings are usually wired.) Tj T* () Tj T* (When choosing a topology, designers weigh cost, the effort of adding devices, the) Tj T* (effect of a failure, and the performance under heavy traffic.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
