BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 4 - The OSI model) Tj T* () Tj T* (To make the design of networks manageable, engineers split the work into layers.) Tj T* (Each layer offers a service to the layer above it and uses the services of the layer) Tj T* (below. The best known reference is the OSI model, published by the International) Tj T* (Organization for Standardization. The OSI model has seven layers: physical, data) Tj T* (link, network, transport, session, presentation and application.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The physical layer deals with signals on the medium: voltages, light pulses and) Tj T* (radio waves. The data link layer moves frames between two directly connected devices) Tj T* (and detects transmission errors. The network layer chooses the path across several) Tj T* (networks and uses logical addresses. The transport layer provides end-to-end) Tj T* (communication between programs, with or without reliability. The session layer) Tj T* (manages dialogues between applications, the presentation layer handles data formats,) Tj T* (encryption and compression, and the application layer is where network services meet) Tj T* (the user.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A popular way to remember the order from the top is the sentence "All People Seem To) Tj T* (Need Data Processing". The model is a teaching tool and a common vocabulary rather) Tj T* (than a description of real software: engineers say that a switch works at layer 2) Tj T* (and a router at layer 3, meaning the data link and network layers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The strength of layering is that a layer can be changed without touching the others.) Tj T* (Wi-Fi can replace a cable at the lower layers, and the web keeps working unchanged) Tj T* (above them.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
