BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 5 - The TCP/IP model) Tj T* () Tj T* (The protocols that run the Internet follow a simpler model with four layers. The) Tj T* (link layer covers the physical connection and the local delivery of frames. The) Tj T* (internet layer, based on the Internet Protocol, handles addressing and routing) Tj T* (across networks. The transport layer contains the two main protocols, TCP and UDP.) Tj T* (The application layer groups everything that programs use directly, such as HTTP,) Tj T* (DNS and SMTP.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The TCP/IP model was developed together with the protocols themselves, whereas the) Tj T* (OSI model was designed first as a general framework. In practice the four-layer) Tj T* (model describes what is really implemented, and the seven-layer model is used for) Tj T* (discussion. The OSI session, presentation and application layers correspond to the) Tj T* (single application layer of TCP/IP, and the OSI physical and data link layers) Tj T* (correspond to its link layer.) Tj T* () Tj T* (One idea explains the success of TCP/IP: the network in the middle stays simple.) Tj T* (Routers only forward packets on a best-effort basis and do not guarantee delivery.) Tj T* (Reliability, ordering and flow control are handled by the hosts at the ends. This) Tj T* (end-to-end principle made it easy to add new applications without changing the core) Tj T* (of the network, which is why the same infrastructure can carry web pages, video) Tj T* (calls and games.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The Internet Engineering Task Force publishes the standards as documents called) Tj T* (RFCs, short for Request for Comments.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
