BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 6 - Encapsulation) Tj T* () Tj T* (When an application sends data, each layer adds its own information in front of the) Tj T* (data, a block called a header. This process is called encapsulation. At the) Tj T* (receiving side, each layer reads and removes its own header before passing the data) Tj T* (up. This is called decapsulation.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The unit of data has a different name at each level. At the transport layer it is a) Tj T* (segment for TCP or a datagram for UDP. At the network layer it is a packet. At the) Tj T* (data link layer it is a frame, and at the physical layer it is simply a stream of) Tj T* (bits. The data created by the application is wrapped in a transport header holding) Tj T* (the port numbers, then in a network header holding the source and destination IP) Tj T* (addresses, then in a link header holding the hardware addresses.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Headers cost space. A tiny message of ten bytes can be carried by a frame several) Tj T* (times larger, and this overhead explains why sending many small messages is less) Tj T* (efficient than sending a few large ones. The link layer also adds a trailer with a) Tj T* (checksum so that the receiver can detect damaged frames.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Encapsulation also explains how devices at different levels can ignore details that) Tj T* (are not theirs. A switch reads only the frame header, a router reads the packet) Tj T* (header, and a web server reads the application data. None of them needs to) Tj T* (understand the layers it does not handle.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
