BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 7 - The physical layer) Tj T* () Tj T* (The physical layer turns bits into signals and carries them over a medium. The most) Tj T* (common wired medium in buildings is twisted pair copper cable, in which pairs of) Tj T* (wires are twisted together to reduce interference. Categories describe its quality.) Tj T* (Category 5e and Category 6 cables support gigabit Ethernet over a maximum length of) Tj T* (100 metres between two devices. Category 6a and above support ten gigabits per) Tj T* (second over the same distance.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Fibre optic cable carries light instead of electricity. It is immune to electrical) Tj T* (interference, supports very high speeds and covers long distances. Multimode fibre) Tj T* (uses a wide core and suits distances of a few hundred metres inside a building or a) Tj T* (data centre. Single-mode fibre uses a very narrow core and can span tens of) Tj T* (kilometres, which is why it is used for links between cities and under the sea.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Wireless media use radio waves, and they share the air between all users in range.) Tj T* (The medium chosen affects speed, cost, range and sensitivity to noise. Coaxial) Tj T* (cable, once the standard for early Ethernet, is now mostly found in cable television) Tj T* (and broadband access.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Signal quality degrades with distance because of attenuation, which is the loss of) Tj T* (signal strength, and because of noise and crosstalk between neighbouring wires.) Tj T* (Repeaters regenerate the signal, and the 100 metre limit for copper exists precisely) Tj T* (because beyond it the signal becomes too weak to be read reliably.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
