BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 8 - Ethernet and MAC addresses) Tj T* () Tj T* (Ethernet is the dominant technology for wired local networks. Every Ethernet) Tj T* (interface has a unique hardware address called a MAC address, which is 48 bits long) Tj T* (and usually written as six pairs of hexadecimal digits, for example) Tj T* (3C:52:82:1A:9F:07. The first half identifies the manufacturer and the second half) Tj T* (identifies the individual card. A special address made of all ones,) Tj T* (FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF, means "every device on this network" and is called the broadcast) Tj T* (address.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Data travels in Ethernet frames. A frame starts with the destination and source MAC) Tj T* (addresses, followed by a type field, the payload and a checksum used to detect) Tj T* (errors. The maximum payload of an Ethernet frame is 1500 bytes, a limit known as the) Tj T* (maximum transmission unit, or MTU. A larger packet must be split into fragments) Tj T* (before it can be sent over Ethernet. The smallest frame is 64 bytes, and shorter) Tj T* (payloads are padded.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Ethernet speeds have grown from ten megabits per second in the early days to ten,) Tj T* (forty and even hundreds of gigabits per second today. Modern links are full duplex,) Tj T* (which means that both sides can send at the same time without colliding. In the) Tj T* (older shared-cable version, devices used a method called CSMA/CD to detect and) Tj T* (recover from collisions, but this is no longer needed on switched networks.) Tj T* () Tj T* (MAC addresses only matter on the local network. When a packet crosses a router, the) Tj T* (frame is rebuilt with new MAC addresses for the next hop.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
