BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 9 - Switches and hubs) Tj T* () Tj T* (A hub is a very simple device that repeats every signal it receives to all its other) Tj T* (ports. All devices connected to a hub share the same bandwidth and the same) Tj T* (collision domain, so performance drops quickly as more devices talk. Hubs are) Tj T* (obsolete today.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A switch is smarter. It reads the destination MAC address of each frame and sends) Tj T* (the frame only to the port where that device is connected. To do this it builds a) Tj T* (MAC address table. When a frame arrives, the switch records the source address and) Tj T* (the port it came from. If the destination is already in the table, the frame goes) Tj T* (out of that single port. If the destination is unknown, or if it is a broadcast, the) Tj T* (switch floods the frame to all ports except the one it arrived on. This simple) Tj T* (learning process needs no configuration.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Because each switch port is its own collision domain, a switch allows several) Tj T* (conversations to happen at the same time at full speed. However, all ports of a) Tj T* (switch still belong to the same broadcast domain, so a broadcast frame reaches every) Tj T* (device. On very large networks, too many broadcasts waste capacity, which is one of) Tj T* (the reasons for splitting the network into VLANs or separate subnets.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Switches can be unmanaged, with no settings at all, or managed, which add features) Tj T* (such as VLANs, monitoring and port security. Redundant links between switches are) Tj T* (protected from loops by the Spanning Tree Protocol.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
