BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 10 - VLANs) Tj T* () Tj T* (A virtual LAN, or VLAN, splits one physical switch into several separate logical) Tj T* (networks. Devices in different VLANs cannot talk to each other directly, even if) Tj T* (they are plugged into the same switch, and broadcasts stay inside their own VLAN.) Tj T* (Companies use VLANs to separate departments, guests and servers without buying extra) Tj T* (hardware, which improves both security and performance.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A port can be an access port, belonging to a single VLAN and connected to an) Tj T* (ordinary device, or a trunk port, which carries the traffic of several VLANs between) Tj T* (switches. To tell the VLANs apart on a trunk, the switch inserts a tag into each) Tj T* (frame, as defined by the IEEE 802.1Q standard. The 802.1Q tag is 4 bytes long, and) Tj T* (12 of its bits hold the VLAN identifier. Twelve bits would give 4096 values, but two) Tj T* (of them are reserved, which leaves 4094 usable VLAN numbers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Traffic that must pass from one VLAN to another needs a router, or a layer 3 switch) Tj T* (that can route. This design is often called router on a stick when a single trunk) Tj T* (link connects the switch to the router. VLANs are only logical: moving a user to) Tj T* (another VLAN is a configuration change and does not require recabling.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A good practice is to leave VLAN 1, the default, unused for user traffic and to give) Tj T* (each VLAN a clear name that describes its purpose.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
