BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 11 - IPv4 addressing) Tj T* () Tj T* (The Internet Protocol gives every interface a logical address. An IPv4 address is 32) Tj T* (bits long and is written as four decimal numbers between 0 and 255 separated by) Tj T* (dots, such as 192.168.1.10. Each number represents eight bits, called an octet. An) Tj T* (address has two parts: the network part, shared by all devices on the same network,) Tj T* (and the host part, which identifies one device.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Some ranges are reserved for private use and are not routed on the public Internet.) Tj T* (These are 10.0.0.0 to 10.255.255.255, 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255, and 192.168.0.0) Tj T* (to 192.168.255.255. Almost every home and office network uses one of them. The) Tj T* (address 127.0.0.1 is the loopback address, which always refers to the machine) Tj T* (itself, and the range starting with 169.254 is used when a device cannot obtain an) Tj T* (address automatically.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The first address of a network identifies the network itself and the last address is) Tj T* (the broadcast address for that network, so neither can be assigned to a host. Public) Tj T* (addresses are handed out by regional registries, and the total supply of IPv4) Tj T* (addresses is limited, which led to widespread use of address translation and to the) Tj T* (creation of a new version of the protocol.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The older system of classes A, B and C, which fixed the size of the network part,) Tj T* (has been replaced by classless addressing. In classless addressing the boundary) Tj T* (between the network and host parts can be placed anywhere, and a prefix length such) Tj T* (as /24 states how many bits belong to the network part.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
