BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 12 - Subnetting) Tj T* () Tj T* (Subnetting splits a large network into smaller ones. A subnet mask, or its shorter) Tj T* (prefix notation, shows which bits belong to the network part. The mask 255.255.255.0) Tj T* (is written /24: the first 24 bits identify the network and the last 8 bits identify) Tj T* (the host. With 8 host bits there are 256 combinations, but two are reserved for the) Tj T* (network and broadcast addresses, so a /24 network offers 254 usable host addresses.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The general rule is that a network with h host bits offers 2 to the power h, minus) Tj T* (2, usable addresses. A /25 network has 7 host bits and therefore 126 usable) Tj T* (addresses. A /26 network has 6 host bits, so it gives 64 addresses in total and 62) Tj T* (usable host addresses. A /27 network gives 30 usable addresses, a /28 gives 14, and) Tj T* (a /30 gives only 2, which is exactly what is needed for a link between two routers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (To split a network, we borrow bits from the host part. Splitting a /24 by borrowing) Tj T* (two bits creates four /26 subnets: for example 192.168.10.0/26, 192.168.10.64/26,) Tj T* (192.168.10.128/26 and 192.168.10.192/26. Each subnet increases by 64 addresses, the) Tj T* (size of the block.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Subnetting has several advantages. It limits the size of broadcast domains, it lets) Tj T* (an administrator apply different rules to different groups of devices, and it uses) Tj T* (the available addresses more efficiently. Variable length subnet masking allows each) Tj T* (subnet to have the size that it really needs.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
