BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 13 - IPv6) Tj T* () Tj T* (IPv6 was designed to replace IPv4 when its addresses started to run out. An IPv6) Tj T* (address is 128 bits long, which gives about 3.4 times ten to the power 38 addresses,) Tj T* (an amount large enough to give a unique address to every device for the foreseeable) Tj T* (future. Addresses are written in hexadecimal as eight groups of four digits) Tj T* (separated by colons, for example 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Because such addresses are long, two shortening rules exist. Leading zeros inside a) Tj T* (group can be dropped, and one single run of groups made only of zeros can be) Tj T* (replaced by a double colon. The example above becomes 2001:db8::1. The double colon) Tj T* (may appear only once in an address, otherwise it would be ambiguous.) Tj T* () Tj T* (IPv6 brings other improvements. The header has a simpler, fixed structure that) Tj T* (routers process faster. There are no broadcasts: multicast is used instead. Devices) Tj T* (can configure their own addresses automatically, and the huge address space removes) Tj T* (the need for address translation, restoring the end-to-end connectivity of the) Tj T* (original Internet. A typical home connection receives a block of addresses from its) Tj T* (provider and each device builds its own address inside it.) Tj T* () Tj T* (IPv4 and IPv6 will coexist for many years. Dual stack hosts run both protocols at) Tj T* (once, and tunnelling and translation mechanisms connect IPv6-only networks with the) Tj T* (IPv4 world.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
