BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 14 - Routing) Tj T* () Tj T* (A router connects different networks and forwards packets between them. To decide) Tj T* (where to send a packet, it consults its routing table, which lists known destination) Tj T* (networks together with the next hop and the outgoing interface for each of them. The) Tj T* (router chooses the entry with the longest matching prefix. When no entry matches,) Tj T* (the packet follows the default route, which usually points to the internet provider.) Tj T* (Hosts use a default gateway, normally the local router, for every destination) Tj T* (outside their own network.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Routes can be configured by hand, which is called static routing and is simple and) Tj T* (predictable on small networks, or learned automatically by routing protocols.) Tj T* (Protocols such as OSPF are used inside one organisation, while the Border Gateway) Tj T* (Protocol, BGP, exchanges routes between the autonomous systems that make up the) Tj T* (Internet.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Every IP packet carries a field called TTL, which means time to live. The TTL field) Tj T* (limits the lifetime of a packet: each router that forwards the packet decreases the) Tj T* (value by one, and when it reaches zero the router discards the packet and sends an) Tj T* (error message back to the sender. This prevents packets from circling forever if a) Tj T* (routing loop occurs. Typical starting values are 64 on Linux systems and 128 on) Tj T* (Windows.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The tool traceroute uses this behaviour on purpose. It sends packets with increasing) Tj T* (TTL values and records which router answers at each step, which reveals the path) Tj T* (across the network.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
