BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 16 - TCP and UDP) Tj T* () Tj T* (The transport layer connects programs rather than machines. The two main protocols) Tj T* (differ in the service they provide. The Transmission Control Protocol, TCP, is) Tj T* (connection-oriented and reliable. It numbers every byte, acknowledges what arrives,) Tj T* (retransmits what is lost, delivers the data in order, and slows down when the) Tj T* (network is congested. The User Datagram Protocol, UDP, is connectionless and) Tj T* (unreliable. It simply sends independent datagrams without any guarantee, which makes) Tj T* (it lighter and faster.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Before exchanging data, TCP opens a connection with a three-way handshake. The) Tj T* (client sends a segment with the SYN flag set, the server replies with SYN-ACK, and) Tj T* (the client confirms with ACK. Only then does data flow. The connection is closed in) Tj T* (an orderly way with FIN segments. The handshake costs one round trip, which is one) Tj T* (reason why short exchanges can feel slow on distant links.) Tj T* () Tj T* (TCP is used when every byte must arrive correctly, for web pages, e-mail and file) Tj T* (transfer. UDP is chosen when speed matters more than perfection or when the) Tj T* (application handles reliability itself, for example voice and video calls, online) Tj T* (games and DNS queries. A lost video frame is better ignored than resent late.) Tj T* () Tj T* (TCP also includes flow control, using a window that tells the sender how much data) Tj T* (the receiver can accept, and congestion control, which reduces the sending rate when) Tj T* (packets are lost.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
