BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 17 - Ports and sockets) Tj T* () Tj T* (An IP address identifies a machine, but a machine runs many programs at once. Port) Tj T* (numbers, which are 16-bit values from 0 to 65535, tell the transport layer which) Tj T* (program should receive the data. The combination of an IP address and a port number) Tj T* (is called a socket, and a connection is identified by the sockets at its two ends.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Ports from 0 to 1023 are the well-known ports, assigned to standard services. The) Tj T* (SSH protocol for secure remote login uses port 22. Telnet uses 23, SMTP uses 25,) Tj T* (HTTP uses 80 and HTTPS uses 443. Ports from 1024 to 49151 are registered ports used) Tj T* (by many applications, and ports from 49152 to 65535 are dynamic, or ephemeral, ports) Tj T* (that a client picks at random for the source of each outgoing connection.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A server program listens on a known port and waits for connections. When a client) Tj T* (connects, the client uses an ephemeral port as its own end, so one server can handle) Tj T* (thousands of clients at once because every connection has a different pair of) Tj T* (sockets.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Knowing the port numbers is useful when configuring firewalls, which allow or block) Tj T* (traffic according to ports, and when diagnosing problems with commands such as) Tj T* (netstat or ss that list the open sockets on a machine.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
