BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 18 - The domain name system) Tj T* () Tj T* (People prefer names to numbers, so the Domain Name System, DNS, translates names) Tj T* (such as www.example.org into IP addresses. DNS is a distributed database organised) Tj T* (as a tree. At the top are the root servers, below them the top-level domains such as) Tj T* (.com, .org or country codes, and below them the authoritative servers that hold the) Tj T* (records of a particular domain. DNS listens on port 53, using UDP for most queries) Tj T* (and TCP for large answers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A program asks a recursive resolver, usually run by the internet provider or a) Tj T* (public service, which does the work of following the tree: it asks a root server) Tj T* (where to find the top-level domain, then asks the top-level server where to find the) Tj T* (domain, and finally asks the authoritative server for the answer. The resolver) Tj T* (returns the result to the client and keeps it in a cache for the time given by the) Tj T* (record's TTL, so repeated questions are answered immediately.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Records have types. An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address and an AAAA record) Tj T* (maps it to an IPv6 address. A CNAME record makes one name an alias of another, an MX) Tj T* (record names the mail servers of a domain, and an NS record names its authoritative) Tj T* (servers. A PTR record performs the reverse lookup from an address to a name.) Tj T* () Tj T* (DNS was originally unprotected, so attackers can try to forge answers. DNSSEC adds) Tj T* (digital signatures to the records to detect such tampering.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
