BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 19 - HTTP and the web) Tj T* () Tj T* (The web is built on the Hypertext Transfer Protocol, HTTP, a request and response) Tj T* (protocol between a browser and a server. The browser sends a request made of a) Tj T* (method, a path, headers and sometimes a body. The server answers with a status code,) Tj T* (headers and the content. The most common methods are GET, which reads a resource,) Tj T* (and POST, which sends data to the server. PUT replaces a resource and DELETE removes) Tj T* (it.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The status code tells the result at a glance. Codes starting with 2 mean success,) Tj T* (and 200 means that everything went well. Codes starting with 3 are redirections,) Tj T* (such as 301 for a permanent move. Codes starting with 4 are client errors: HTTP) Tj T* (status code 404 means that the requested resource was not found on the server, and) Tj T* (403 means that access is forbidden. Codes starting with 5 are server errors, such as) Tj T* (500 for an internal error and 503 when the service is unavailable.) Tj T* () Tj T* (HTTP is stateless: the server does not remember previous requests, so websites use) Tj T* (cookies to recognise a returning visitor. Resources are named by URLs, which combine) Tj T* (the protocol, the host name, an optional port and the path.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The protocol has evolved. HTTP/1.1 opened one request after another on a connection,) Tj T* (HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests over a single connection to load pages faster, and) Tj T* (HTTP/3 runs over a transport called QUIC, which is built on UDP and reduces the) Tj T* (delay caused by lost packets.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
