BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 20 - Electronic mail) Tj T* () Tj T* (Electronic mail uses several protocols, each with its own job. A mail client sends a) Tj T* (message to its provider with the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol, SMTP. Mail servers) Tj T* (also use SMTP to relay the message to the recipient's server. SMTP between servers) Tj T* (uses port 25, while clients submitting mail normally use port 587 with) Tj T* (authentication and encryption.) Tj T* () Tj T* (To read the mail, the recipient's client fetches it from the server. With POP3, the) Tj T* (Post Office Protocol, the client downloads the messages and usually removes them) Tj T* (from the server, which is simple but ties the mailbox to one device. With IMAP, the) Tj T* (Internet Message Access Protocol, the messages stay on the server and the client) Tj T* (shows a synchronised view, so the same mailbox looks identical on a phone and a) Tj T* (laptop. IMAP uses port 143, or 993 when protected by TLS, and POP3 uses 110, or 995) Tj T* (when protected.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The destination servers are found through MX records in the DNS. If several MX) Tj T* (records exist, each has a priority and the sender tries the lowest number first. A) Tj T* (message that cannot be delivered is kept in a queue and retried for some days before) Tj T* (an error is returned to the sender.) Tj T* () Tj T* (E-mail was designed in a time of trust, so unwanted messages are a constant problem.) Tj T* (Techniques such as SPF, DKIM and DMARC let a receiving server check that a message) Tj T* (really comes from the domain it claims to come from.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
