BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 21 - Network security) Tj T* () Tj T* (Because networks carry sensitive data, several mechanisms protect them. A firewall) Tj T* (filters traffic according to rules, for example by address and port. A stateful) Tj T* (firewall remembers established connections and automatically allows the replies,) Tj T* (while a packet filter judges each packet alone. A demilitarised zone, or DMZ, is a) Tj T* (separate network segment for servers that must be reachable from outside, so that a) Tj T* (compromised server does not give access to the internal network.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Encryption protects the content. The Transport Layer Security protocol, TLS, secures) Tj T* (protocols such as HTTPS. TLS 1.3, standardised in 2018, is the current version.) Tj T* (During the handshake the server proves its identity with a digital certificate) Tj T* (signed by a certificate authority, and the two sides agree on keys that are then) Tj T* (used to encrypt the data. Certificates are what the padlock in a browser stands for.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A virtual private network, VPN, creates an encrypted tunnel across an untrusted) Tj T* (network such as the Internet, so remote users can reach a company network as if they) Tj T* (were inside it. Common technologies include IPsec, OpenVPN and WireGuard.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Security also depends on people and habits: strong and unique passwords, multi-) Tj T* (factor authentication, regular updates, and the principle of least privilege, which) Tj T* (gives every user and program only the access it needs. Monitoring and logging help) Tj T* (detect attacks early, and backups limit the damage when an attack succeeds.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
