BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 22 - Wireless networks) Tj T* () Tj T* (Wireless local networks follow the IEEE 802.11 family of standards, marketed as Wi-) Tj T* (Fi. They work in unlicensed radio bands, mainly 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz, and newer devices) Tj T* (also use 6 GHz. The 2.4 GHz band reaches further and passes through walls better,) Tj T* (but it is crowded and offers only a few channels. It has channels 1 to 13 in most) Tj T* (countries, but they are so close together that neighbouring channels overlap. The) Tj T* (three channels 1, 6 and 11 do not overlap, so access points close to each other) Tj T* (should use them to avoid interference.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The standards have improved through the years. 802.11b offered 11 Mbit/s, 802.11g) Tj T* (reached 54 Mbit/s, 802.11n introduced several antennas, MIMO, and 802.11ac) Tj T* (concentrated on the 5 GHz band. Wi-Fi 6, which is the name for IEEE 802.11ax,) Tj T* (reaches a theoretical maximum of 9.6 Gbit/s and is designed to work better when many) Tj T* (devices are connected at once. Real speeds are always lower than the theoretical) Tj T* (maximum because the air is shared and signals weaken with distance and obstacles.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A wireless network is identified by its SSID, the name that users see. Wi-Fi traffic) Tj T* (can be intercepted by anyone in range, so it must be encrypted. The old WEP method) Tj T* (is broken and WPA2 has long been the standard, while WPA3, introduced in 2018,) Tj T* (strengthens the protection of passwords.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Good coverage depends on the position of the access points, which should be central) Tj T* (and high, and on avoiding sources of interference such as microwave ovens.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
