BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 23 - Troubleshooting tools) Tj T* () Tj T* (When a network does not work, a method helps more than guessing. A common approach) Tj T* (is to work from the bottom up: first check the cable and the lights, then the) Tj T* (address configuration, then the connection to the gateway, and finally the name) Tj T* (resolution and the application.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The ping command sends ICMP echo requests to a host and measures how long the) Tj T* (replies take. It shows whether the host is reachable and how much delay and packet) Tj T* (loss there is. The traceroute command, called tracert on Windows, lists the routers) Tj T* (along the path by using the TTL field, and it shows where a connection stops or) Tj T* (slows down. The ipconfig command on Windows and ip addr on Linux display the) Tj T* (addresses of the local interfaces.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The nslookup and dig commands query the DNS and help to find out whether a problem) Tj T* (comes from the name resolution. The netstat and ss commands show open connections) Tj T* (and listening ports. For deeper analysis, a packet analyser such as Wireshark) Tj T* (captures the traffic and displays each frame decoded layer by layer, which is) Tj T* (excellent for learning how protocols really behave.) Tj T* () Tj T* (It is useful to write down what was changed and what the result was. Changing one) Tj T* (thing at a time makes it possible to find the real cause, and comparing the) Tj T* (situation with a known working configuration often reveals the difference at once.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
