BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td 14 TL (Chapter 24 - Summary and glossary) Tj T* () Tj T* (This last chapter brings the main ideas together. A network is a set of devices that) Tj T* (exchange data using shared protocols. The work is divided into layers, and each) Tj T* (layer adds a header to the data as it travels down the stack at the sender and) Tj T* (removes it at the receiver. Local delivery relies on hardware addresses and) Tj T* (switches, while delivery between networks relies on IP addresses and routers.) Tj T* (Transport protocols connect programs through port numbers, and applications such as) Tj T* (the web and e-mail use names resolved by the DNS.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Short glossary. Bandwidth is the maximum amount of data a link can carry per second.) Tj T* (Latency is the time a message takes to travel from the sender to the receiver.) Tj T* (Throughput is the speed actually achieved. A protocol is a set of rules for) Tj T* (communication. A packet is a unit of data at the network layer. A gateway is a) Tj T* (device that connects a network to others. A firewall is a filter for traffic. An) Tj T* (autonomous system is a network under a single administrative control.) Tj T* () Tj T* (To go further, build a small lab with virtual machines, capture the traffic with a) Tj T* (packet analyser and compare it with what these chapters describe. Reading the) Tj T* (original RFC documents is a good next step for each protocol, and practising) Tj T* (subnetting with a few examples every day quickly makes it automatic.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Understanding networks takes time, but the reward is large: almost every modern) Tj T* (service depends on the ideas covered in these notes.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
